      <item>
        <title>RT by @elonmusk: SpaceX just completed its 90th Falcon-family launch of 2026 Falcon 9 lifted off from Vandenberg carrying 24 Starlink satellites, successfully deploying all of them into low Earth orbit Booster B1081 completed its 26th mission and landed on Of Course I Still Love You in the Pacific That is 89 Falcon 9 launches and one Falcon Heavy mission in just seven months</title>
        <dc:creator>@XFreeze</dc:creator>
        <description><![CDATA[<p>SpaceX just completed its 90th Falcon-family launch of 2026<br>
<br>
Falcon 9 lifted off from Vandenberg carrying 24 Starlink satellites, successfully deploying all of them into low Earth orbit<br>
<br>
Booster B1081 completed its 26th mission and landed on Of Course I Still Love You in the Pacific<br>
<br>
That is 89 Falcon 9 launches and one Falcon Heavy mission in just seven months</p>
<img src="https://nitter.net/pic/media%2FHOnjpgSWQAAne70.jpg" style="max-width:250px;" />]]></description>
        <pubDate>Sat, 01 Aug 2026 06:57:13 GMT</pubDate>
        <guid isPermaLink="false">2083446940562243827</guid>
        <link>https://nitter.net/XFreeze/status/2083446940562243827#m</link>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>The Moon base will be even more epic</title>
        <dc:creator>@elonmusk</dc:creator>
        <description><![CDATA[<p>The Moon base will be even more epic</p>
<hr/>
<blockquote>
<b>Don Pettit (@astro_Pettit)</b>
<p>
<p>Many have asked how this photo was made. This is my explanation.<br>
<br>
The ISS rotates about its pitch axis as it orbits Earth at 17,000mph to keep its nadir view constant. Because of this long exposure photography turns stars into arced lines.<br>
<br>
Sidereal clock drives on Earth match the planet's rotation to see stars clearly. I knew one designed for space could do this too.<br>
<br>
So a camera mount was custom built at <a href="https://nitter.net/RITtigers" title="RIT">@RITtigers</a> to rotate once every 90 minutes using a 60 to 90 toothed gear system, cancelling out the orbital motion of ISS and allowing the capture of distant stars as fixed points! <br>
<br>
In essence, it is a a rotational coil spring powered analog star tracker. It was machined out of aluminum and is entirely mechanical to bypass NASA's strict electrical requirements for cargo. We had to get creative!<br>
<br>
After Expedition 72 it was safely returned to Earth and is to be displayed at RIT. Which I am pleased to say because a similar barn door tracker I made from spare parts on Expedition 6 for nighttime city photography was destroyed in an atmospheric trash disposal!<br>
<br>
Photography is an important part of sharing the journey to new frontiers for all people of Earth!</p>
<img src="https://nitter.net/pic/media%2FHOqhMYlWwAEKtwv.jpg" style="max-width:250px;" />


</p>
<footer>
— <cite><a href="https://nitter.net/astro_Pettit/status/2083655015240016368#m">https://nitter.net/astro_Pettit/status/2083655015240016368#m</a>
</footer>
</blockquote>]]></description>
        <pubDate>Sat, 01 Aug 2026 20:57:09 GMT</pubDate>
        <guid isPermaLink="false">2083658315582632272</guid>
        <link>https://nitter.net/elonmusk/status/2083658315582632272#m</link>
